# The ledger of `sql/odbc`: any database the system's ODBC driver manager
# reaches, measured against three libraries in the author's choice of
# 2026-11-17. .NET's `System.Data.Odbc` (version 11.0.0: every member its
# NuGet package's `lib/net11.0/System.Data.Odbc.xml` documents, 267 names,
# read 2026-11-18, since no .NET runs on this machine); Python's `pyodbc`
# (5.2.0, Debian's `python3-pyodbc` in the Linux arm64 container: `dir()` of
# the module, of `Connection`, of `Cursor` and of `Row`, 301, 18, 31 and 1
# names, 2026-11-18), the yardstick; and Go's `database/sql` driver for ODBC,
# `github.com/alexbrainman/odbc` (the version of 2025-06-01, `go doc -all`
# in a module of the scratch folder), the yardstick. Rust's standard library
# holds no ODBC, so its lines are `-`. Go's `database/sql` itself stands in
# the ledgers of `sql` and `sql/postgres`, whose API this driver keeps name
# for name.
#
# pyodbc's 261 `SQL_*` constants stand in blocks by their use: 175 items of
# `getinfo`, 40 SQL types, 24 attributes of a connection, 4 isolation
# levels, 8 kinds of parameter and 9 answers of the special-columns
# question. Expected values of the tests were observed on 2026-11-18 from
# unixODBC 2.3.12 with SQLite's ODBC driver 0.99991 (SQLite 3.46.1) and
# PostgreSQL's 17.00.0004 against PostgreSQL 17, in the Linux arm64
# container, by this package's member probe and by pyodbc.

capability odbc/manager  which driver manager the program loaded, and none told apart
  heroes  sql/odbc.manager
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  odbcversion, SQL_DM_VER
  dotnet  -
  note    the library's name, `libodbc.so.2` or `odbc32.dll`; with none installed every call fails `unsupported`, the message naming each library tried. Windows' `odbc32.dll` is loaded from the system's folder alone. The manager's version is `Info.manager_version`

capability odbc/drivers  the drivers the manager knows, with their configuration
  heroes  sql/odbc.drivers, sql/odbc.Driver
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  drivers
  dotnet  -
  note    each driver with its attributes in the configuration's order, where pyodbc answers the names alone

capability odbc/data-sources  the data sources the manager knows by name
  heroes  sql/odbc.data_sources, sql/odbc.DataSource
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  dataSources
  dotnet  -
  note    the user's then the system's, each saying which, where pyodbc answers one dictionary of names to drivers

capability odbc/open  a connection made from a connection string, or to a data source by its name
  heroes  sql/odbc.open, sql/odbc.options, sql/odbc.dsn_options, sql/odbc.Options, sql/odbc.Target, sql/odbc.Db
  go      Driver, Driver.Open, Conn
  rust    -
  python  connect, Connection
  dotnet  OdbcConnection.#ctor, OdbcConnection.ConnectionString, OdbcConnection.Open, OdbcConnection.DataSource, OdbcFactory, OdbcFactory.Instance, OdbcFactory.CreateConnection
  note    `SQLDriverConnect` with no prompt for a connection string, `SQLConnect` for a data source with its user and password, as .NET and pyodbc connect; pyodbc's keywords are pairs of the connection string, written by `format_connection`. A message names a connection string with every password written `***`

capability odbc/login-timeout  how long a connection waits to log in
  heroes  sql/odbc.Options, sql/odbc.DEFAULT_LOGIN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  connect timeout, SQL_ATTR_LOGIN_TIMEOUT, SQL_LOGIN_TIMEOUT
  dotnet  OdbcConnection.ConnectionTimeout
  note    fifteen seconds unless told, .NET's default (its documentation: "The default value is 15 seconds")

capability odbc/close  a connection ended, and asked whether it is
  heroes  sql/odbc.close, sql/odbc.is_closed
  go      Conn.Close, Driver.Close
  rust    -
  python  Connection.close, Connection.closed
  dotnet  OdbcConnection.Close, OdbcConnection.State
  note    a transaction still open is rolled back, as ODBC's `SQLDisconnect` requires; `close` refuses while a statement `prepare` made is open, as `sql/sqlite` does. The layer ends a connection's statements with it whatever the face does, so no statement's handle outlives its connection

capability odbc/ping  whether a connection still works, by its driver's own knowledge
  heroes  sql/odbc.ping
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  dotnet  OdbcConnection.State
  note    `SQL_ATTR_CONNECTION_DEAD`, which asks no statement of the database; a driver that cannot tell counts as working

capability odbc/exec  one statement run with its values bound as parameters, and what it changed
  heroes  sql/odbc.exec
  go      Stmt.Exec, Result, Result.RowsAffected, Result.LastInsertId
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.execute, Connection.execute, Cursor.rowcount
  dotnet  OdbcCommand, OdbcCommand.#ctor, OdbcCommand.CommandText, OdbcCommand.Connection, OdbcCommand.ExecuteNonQuery, OdbcConnection.CreateCommand, OdbcFactory.CreateCommand, OdbcDataReader.RecordsAffected
  note    `rows_affected` is the driver's count, 0 where it says none (pyodbc answers -1), and the rows of a statement that answers some, read and counted (SQLite's driver counts 0 for a `SELECT`). `last_id` fails `unsupported`: ODBC keeps none, and Go's driver answers an error too. Text after the first statement is refused `invalid`, which SQLite's driver also refuses ("only one SQL statement allowed") and others would run

